# Spec workflow
The user writes the spec. The agent implements it. The user approves the result.
## When the workflow applies
The workflow starts only when the user sends `/implement <id> [N]`, or names a task id or a spec file and asks to implement it.
The workflow does not apply to other chat messages. Review feedback, fixes, questions, small edits, and investigations sent in chat are done directly. Do not ask for a spec. Do not create `.spec` files for them.
## Rules
- The spec is `.spec/tasks/<id>/spec.md`. A person writes it. Use `.cursor/skills/spec-implement/spec-template.md` as the shape.
- When the user gives a brief or a feature request for a new task and no `spec.md` exists, do not write product code. Do not write the spec. Say that the spec is missing, point to the template, and wait. Write or edit the spec only when the user asks for it.
- Every subtask in the spec has a Definition, Recommendations, and a Verification approach. When one is missing, stop and name the subtask and the missing part.
- `/implement <id>` implements all subtasks in order. `/implement <id> N` implements subtask N only. Follow `.cursor/skills/spec-implement/implement.md`.
- Each subtask gets its own file `.spec/tasks/<id>/subtask-N.md` with its status and progress log. Create it before the first product edit for that subtask.
- Do not invent behavior that the spec and the code do not support. When the spec is unclear or conflicts with the code, stop and ask.
- Do not change the spec while implementing. When it must change, propose the change and wait for the user.
- Only the user approves. The agent sets a finished subtask to `ready-for-review`, never to `approved`. The agent sets `approved` only after the user says so for that subtask.
- Do not edit reviewer rules, the PMD ruleset, ArchUnit tests, or `.spec/docs/baseline.md` to turn a check green.
